    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d812">

                <pb facs="0780" n="770" sameAs="frus:frus1941v05/pg770"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/7372</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Chief of the Division of Far Eastern
                        Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Hamilton</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-12-31">December 31, 1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Secretary</hi>: If Manila falls soon to the
                    Japanese, the effect on the morale of the Chinese and of other native peoples in
                    the Far Eastern area is bound to be adverse.</p>
                <p>With regard to China, I think it important that this Government be prepared to
                    grant promptly to the Chinese Government a substantial loan (Ambassador Gauss
                    reported in his telegram no. 548 of December 30 that Chiang Kai-shek had
                    requested such a loan<anchor n="65" xml:id="d812-f1"/>). I suggest that you may
                    care to speak to the President in regard to this matter.</p>
                <p>With regard to other native peoples in the Far East (the natives of Malaya, the
                    Dutch East Indies, India, Burma), I think it important that Colonel
                        Donovan’s<anchor n="66" xml:id="d812-f2"/> organization put forth unusual
                    and special effort to disseminate propaganda favorable to our cause. I suggest
                    that you might care to speak to the President on this point with a view to the
                    President’s possibly speaking to Colonel Donovan.<anchor n="67" xml:id="d812-f3"
                    />
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>M[<hi rendition="#small-caps">axwell</hi>] M. H[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">amilton</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d812-f1" n="65" xml:id="d812-f1-content">
                <p>Notation by Mr. Hamilton: “Mr. Berle is having studied the question of a loan. M.
                    M. H.”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d812-f2" n="66" xml:id="d812-f2-content">
                <p>Col. William J. Donovan, Coordinator of Information.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d812-f3" n="67" xml:id="d812-f3-content">
                <p>Notation by the Adviser on Political Relations (Hornbeck): “Concur. S. K. H.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
